cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below. If you use the theoretical argument, please also cite the accompanying paper."
title: "Blind by Design: Computational Supplement"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Kahl"
    given-names: "Peter"
    affiliation: "Independent researcher, Lex et Ratio"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-1616-4843"
version: "0.2.0"
date-released: "2026-10-07"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Peter-Kahl/blind-by-design-qec"
url: "https://www.lexetratio.com"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Computational supplement to 'Blind by Design: Quantum Error Correction,
  Functional Incompatibility and the Value of Evidence' (Lex et Ratio Working
  Paper LXR-2026-PHI-BBDQEC-WP). Five scripts: a numerical check of
  Proposition 5.2, which bounds what the classical record of an approximately
  function-preserving inquiry can reveal about an encoded quantum state; a
  three-qubit repetition-code
  instrument showing observation-relative and function-relative blindness; an
  independent numerical check of a toric-code sign-symmetry result (Özgüler
  2026, Proposition 1); a construction audit of Stim/PyMatching surface-code
  simulations; and a continuous error-correction simulation in which a
  misspecified detector efficiency is detected and repaired from the records
  already held. Includes reference outputs and a reliance note.
keywords:
  - "quantum error correction"
  - "functional incompatibility"
  - "value of evidence"
  - "syndrome measurement"
  - "detector error models"
  - "model misspecification"
  - "continuous measurement"
  - "philosophy of science"
  - "information-disturbance trade-off"
  - "diamond norm"
